.TH RXENUM 1 "Jan 2012" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
rxenum \- Enumerate or count the size of sets specified by regular expressions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rxenum
[\fIoptions\fR]
[\fIregex\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rxenum
looks at the (mostly Perl-compatible) regular expression specified in
.B regex
as the specification of a set of elements. By default, it counts the
number of elements in this set and displays it to the user as an exact
decimal number with as many digits as needed (it is easy to generate
very large numbers).

For example, let's compute how many eight alphanumeric character passwords
there are:

.RS
rxenum '[0-9A-Za-z]{8}'
.br
218,340,105,584,896
.br
~ 10^14.3391
.br
~  2^47.6336
.RE

As regular expressions often contain characters with special meaning for the
shell, it is recommended to enclose them in single quotes.

If invoked with \fB-n\fR, \fBrxenum\fR will explicitly generate and print every
single element in the set, numbering them along the way. If you add \fB-z\fR,
the numbering starts from zero instead of one.

For example, let's enumerate roman numerals:

.RS
rxenum -zn 'M{0,3}(C{0,3}|CD|DC{0,3}|CM)'\\
.br
           '(X{0,3}|XL|LX{0,3}|XC)(I{0,3}|IV|VI{0,3}|IX)'
.br
    0
.br
    1 I
.br
    2 II
.br
    3 III
.br
    4 IV
.br
    ( ... thousands of lines omitted for brevity ... )
.br
3,998 MMMCMXCVIII
.br
3,999 MMMCMXCIX
.RE

As the example above shows, enumerations establish a mapping from the integers
to the elements of the set. We can use this to jump directly to some nth
element without having to enumerate all the previous ones. The example below
shows how this can be used to convert a number from decimal to hexadecimal:

.RS
rxenum -z -f 3735928559 '([0-9A-F]{4} ){2}'
.br
DEAD BEEF
.RE

We can also have the element chosen at random. For instance, let's choose
a sort-of-pronounceable random password using the koremutake method:

.RS
rxenum -r '(([bdfghjklmnprstv]|[bdfgp]r|st)[aeiouy]|tra|tre){4}'
.br
supustytra
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
If no switches are specified,
.B rxenum
will count the number of elements in the set specified by
.B regex
and display the count in three forms: as an arbitrary precision
exact integer, as an truncated approximate power of ten and a truncated
approximated power of two. When the approximations turn out to be exact,
the powers are preceded by an equal sign; otherwise, they will be preceded
by a tilde.
.TP
.B
\-i
Case insensitive. "a" stands for "[Aa]", "b" for "[Bb]" and so on.
Corresponds to the /i flag in Perl regexes.
.TP
.B
\-s
Makes the dot quantifier really match all characters instead of "all but
newline". Corresponds to the /s "single line" flag in Perl regexes.
.TP
.B
\-L
Enumerate left to right, so that the first position in the string varies
fastest instead of the last. See ENUMERATION ORDER below.
.TP
.B
\-e
Enumerate the set, generating and printing the element instead of just
counting them.
.TP
.B
\-n
Number the elements of the set. Implies \fB-e\fR.
.TP
.B
\-z
("zero-offset") Start numbering from zero instead of 1.
.TP
.B
\-c number
("count") Stop the enumeration after
.B
number
\, items have been displayed.
.B
number
may be an arbitrarily large integer.  Implies \fB-e\fR.
.TP
.B
\-f number
("from") Start the enumeration from the element specified in
.B
number
\, which may be an arbitrarily large integer limited only by the set size.
If
.B
number
is larger than the number of elements in the set, a "seek past end" error
will be displayed. The first element is zero if
.B
\-z
is also specified or 1 otherwise. If
.B
-f
is not specified, it will default to the first element.  Implies \fB-e\fR.
.TP
.B
\-t number
("to") Specifies the final element of the enumeration. More precisely,
computes the element count (see option 
.B
-c
\) as number minus the starting point (see option
-f
\) plus one.  Implies \fB-e\fR.
.TP
.B
\-k key
Walk the set in an order that depends on
.B key
instead of in index order, visiting every member exactly once. See
PERMUTED ORDER below. Implies \fB-e\fR, and cannot be combined with \fB-r\fR.
.TP
.B
\-Q
Print which order the set is enumerated in -- "shortlex", "diagonal" or
"place value" -- and do nothing else. See ENUMERATION ORDER and INFINITE SETS.
.TP
.B
\-r
Randomly choose an element of the set. You can get several random choices
by setting
.B
-c
as well.
.TP
.B
\-,
Uses the 'comma' character as the thousands separator.
.TP
.B
\-.
Uses the 'dot' character as the thousands separator.
.TP
.B
\-_
Uses the 'underscore' character as the thousands separator.
.TP
.B
\-~
Use no thousands separator at all.

.SH ENUMERATION ORDER
By default the enumeration runs right to left: the last position in the
string is the least significant one, so it varies fastest, exactly as the
last digit of an ordinary numeral does. That is what allows the mapping to
be used for radix conversion:

.RS
rxenum -z -f 3735928559 '([0-9A-F]{4} ){2}'
.br
DEAD BEEF
.RE

The
.B -L
option, or the non-standard "(?L)" flag written inside the regex, reverses
this so the first position varies fastest:

.RS
rxenum -L -z -f 3735928559 '([0-9A-F]{4} ){2}'
.br
FEEB DAED
.RE

The flag is inherited by subexpressions and by repetitions, and "(?-L)"
turns it off again, so the direction can be chosen independently for any
part of a regex:

.RS
rxenum -e '(?L)[ab](?-L:[cd][ef])'
.br
ace bce acf bcf ade bde adf bdf
.RE

Only the order changes. The set and its size are identical either way.

Alternations are always enumerated in the order they are written and are
not affected by this flag. To reverse them, write them the other way round.

Note that L is not a Perl flag. Every inline flag Perl defines is lower
case, so an upper case letter cannot collide with one; lower case l was not
available, Perl having given it to locale rules in version 5.14.

.SH INFINITE SETS
The star and plus metacharacters, and open-ended repetitions such as
"a{1,}", describe sets with no largest member. There is no count to print,
so \fBrxenum\fR says so:

.RS
rxenum 'a*'
.br
infinite
.RE

They are still enumerated, and still addressed by index, so everything but
the counting works as it does for a finite set. Use \fB-c\fR to say how many
elements you want:

.RS
rxenum -e -c 5 '[ab]+'
.br
a b aa ab ba
.RE

The mapping is a bijection onto the whole set: every member is reached after
finitely many steps, and \fB-f\fR addresses the same order, so a run can be
stopped and resumed exactly as a finite one can.

.RS
rxenum -z -f 1000 'a*b*'
.br
aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaabbbbbbbbbb
.RE

Members come out shortest first. Within a length, the more significant
positions take as much of it as they can before the less significant ones do,
and within a given division the ordinary place value applies:

.RS
rxenum -e -c 10 'a*b*'
.br
(empty) a b aa ab bb aaa aab abb bbb
.RE

That order is what makes an infinite set practical rather than merely
enumerable. \fBrxenum\fR works out how many members an expression has of each
length rather than in total, which a cardinality cannot say, and then walks
the lengths in turn. The counts are worked out only as far as they are asked
for, so an expression such as "[a-z]{1,20000}a*" costs nothing until you
enumerate strings twenty thousand characters long.

A comma-separated list is the shape where this tells:

.RS
rxenum -e -c 6 '(\\d+,)*'
.br
(empty) 0, 1, 2, 3, 4,
.RE

with the first two-element list at index 1,111, after the one empty string,
the ten one-digit lists, the hundred two-digit ones and the thousand
three-digit ones.

One shape is refused: an unbounded repetition whose body can match nothing at
all, as in "(a*)*" or "(a?)+". Such an expression derives the empty string
infinitely many ways, so the number of members of a given length is not a
number and there is nothing to count. Perl's matcher refuses an empty loop
body for the same reason. The restriction is narrow, and deliberately so:
"(\\d+,)*" spends at least two characters per repetition and is perfectly
countable.

A backreference is the exception to all of the above. Its length is whatever
the group it names turned out to be, so the positions of a concatenation stop
being independent and their lengths can no longer be counted separately. Such
an expression is enumerated in a different order -- a diagonal walk over the
unbounded quantifiers, which still reaches every member in finite time but
not shortest first.

.B -r
and
.B -k
both need a finite set to work over and report an error otherwise.

.SH PERMUTED ORDER
.B \-k
composes the integer mapping with a keyed permutation of the integers, so
the set is enumerated in a scattered but perfectly reproducible order:

.RS
rxenum -k hunter2 -e '[a-c][x-z]'
.br
cx az by bz cy cz ay ax bx
.RE

The same key always gives the same order, and a different key gives a
different one. Because it is a permutation rather than a sample, every
member appears exactly once: unlike
.B -r
it cannot repeat itself and cannot miss anything.

The numbering still addresses this order, so a run can be resumed. Element
zero of the run above is "cx", and:

.RS
rxenum -k hunter2 -z -f 4 '[a-c][x-z]'
.br
cy
.RE

which is the fifth line printed. That makes it practical to randomise a
brute force search and still stop and continue it later, or to split one
across several machines by handing each a range of indices.

The permutation is built from a Feistel network over the index, walked back
into range when it lands outside. It is not a cipher and does not try to be:
it exists to spread the search order, not to withstand somebody who wants to
recover the key. The key is folded to 64 bits however long it is.

The order produced by a given key is fixed and is treated as part of the
interface, since reproducibility is the whole point of using a key.

.SH OTHER EXAMPLES
.TP
((\\d|[1-9]\\d|1\\d\\d|2[0-4]\\d|25[0-5])\\.){3}(?2)
Generates all 4,294,967,296 possible IPv4 addresses. Notice the usage of the
(?2) construct to reuse the second parenthesized subexpression as a kind of
"subroutine".
.TP
(([01]\\d\\d|2[0-4]\\d|25[0-5]|\\d{1,2})\\.){3}(?2)
Alternative formulation that also handles zero-padded numbers. Because of
that, there are 17,944,209,936 items in this set instead of 4,294,967,296.

.SH CAVEATS
All regular expressions are assumed to be anchored, as if they were
preceded by ^ and terminated by $. In fact, a ^ in the beginning is ignored,
as is a dollar sign at the end. Anywhere else, they are treated as literals.

The
.B -i
and
.B -s
flags can also be set inside the regex itself, either for the remainder of
the enclosing group as in "(?i)abc", or for a single subexpression as in
"(?i:abc)". A leading minus turns them off again, as in "(?-i)". A group
that only sets flags does not count as a capturing group, so it does not
consume a backreference number. The /m flag is accepted and ignored: it
governs where ^ and $ match, and those are not honoured anywhere, as noted
above.

Absolute backreferences are supported, but relative ones are not.

A backreference to a group repeated a fixed number of times refers to the
last repetition, as in Perl. So "([ab]){2}\\1" gives aaa, abb, baa and bbb.

A backreference to a group repeated a variable number of times, such as
"([ab]){1,2}\\1", is currently rejected, because the number of repetitions is
not fixed and so neither is which one the backreference should copy.
Reformulate it, for instance as "([ab])\\1|([ab]){2}\\2", to say explicitly which
repetition you mean.

To emulate Perl behavior, two sucessive quantifiers (say, "a?{2}") gives
an error. And again just like Perl, you can easily work around it by using
subexpressions, say, "(a?){2}".

.SH LIMITATIONS
Works only in 8-bit characters and ASCII. No Unicode support.

Neither detects nor skips duplicates:

.RS
rxenum -n '(a?){2}'
.br
1
.br
2 a
.br
3 a
.br
4 aa
.RE

A generated element is truncated at 2048 characters, without saying so. Wide
and unbounded repetitions are counted and seeked correctly but cannot be
printed in full past that: "a{1,100000}" and "a*" both hit it.

POSIX character classes are currently unimplemented, as are many backslash
special characters. Named backreferences are also currently unimplemented.

